    let b = arg.as_bytes();
    if b.len() > 1 && b[0] == b'-' {
        usage_exit();
    }
    if b == b"--" {
        after_ddash = true;
        continue;
    }
}

// AFTER (fixed):
if !after_ddash {
    let b = arg.as_bytes();
    if b == b"--" {
        after_ddash = true;
        continue;
    }
    if b.len() > 1 && b[0] == b'-' {
        usage_exit();
    }
}
```

## 3. Compiler diagnostics

None. The change is a pure reordering of two existing statements; no new types, imports, or variables are introduced.

## 4. How the edits address the reported failure

With the corrected order, when the argument is `--`, the `b == b"--"` branch fires first, sets `after_ddash = true`, and `continue`s. All subsequent arguments (including `-alpha`) skip the `!after_ddash` block entirely and are assigned to `pattern` or `files` as ordinary operands. The unsupported-option rejection still fires for any other dash-prefixed token encountered before `--` (e.g. `-r`, `--color`), preserving the original "reject unsupported options" requirement.

